{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## LINK TYPES DESERVE THEIR OWN SECTION — a name-only check cannot catch the failure that matters\n\nAlready in the broadened checklist, but under-specified there. The actual map is:\n\n    RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK: dict[str, tuple[str, bool]] = {\n        \"blocks\":      (\"Blocks\",  False),\n        \"depends_on\":  (\"Blocks\",  True),   # A depends_on B == B blocks A\n        \"relates_to\":  (\"Relates\", False),\n    }\n\n**`blocks` and `depends_on` map to the SAME Jira link type and are distinguished ONLY by that\nboolean.** So a direction error does not produce a missing link or an error — it produces a link\nthat exists, looks right, and **points the wrong way**. A dependency silently inverts. Checking that\nthe type name `\"Blocks\"` exists on the instance would pass with the direction completely wrong,\nwhich is why this needs more than the presence check the other three vocabularies need.\n\nThe place it hides: Jira link types carry THREE strings, not one — the type `name` (`\"Blocks\"`) plus\ndistinct `inward` and `outward` names (`\"is blocked by\"` / `\"blocks\"`). Writing a link uses the type\nname and a direction; READING one back inbound has to interpret the inward/outward name. A\nmismatch between how we write and how we read is invisible to any single-ended check, and rebar\nalready has an 89-line `link_direction.py` devoted to this hazard.\n\n### What the map job must capture\n\n * `/rest/api/2/issueLinkType` in full: for every type, its `name`, `inward` and `outward` strings —\n   not just whether `\"Blocks\"` and `\"Relates\"` exist\n * an **end-to-end direction experiment**, because this cannot be settled by reading config: create\n   A and B, write a `blocks` link A→B through rebar, then read the raw issue JSON for BOTH A and B\n   and record which side reports which of the inward/outward names. Repeat for `depends_on` and\n   confirm it lands as the INVERSE, not a duplicate of `blocks`\n * the same read-back for `relates_to`, whose symmetry means a direction bug is undetectable from\n   the link alone and would only surface as a diff that never converges\n\n### A gap the map should also record\n\nOnly **three** of rebar's seven link relations are mapped: `blocks`, `depends_on`, `relates_to`.\n`duplicates`, `supersedes`, `discovered_from` and `caused_by` have no Jira equivalent here — so\npresumably they do not sync at all. The map should record which of those the instance COULD express\n(`Duplicate` and `Cloners` ship with stock Jira), so the \"not synced\" state is a documented decision\nrather than an accident nobody has looked at. That is a finding to file, not to fix in the map job."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785798450290210001,"uuid":"9001048d-06b1-4017-9651-c829f69c2149"}